(function () {
  if (typeof BRANDS_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var EN = {
    "Meerapfel": {
      founded: "1876 (the year the family's cigar factory was founded at Untergrombach, Germany; the family's tobacco cultivation and trading, however, is said to reach back much further, to the 1600s, or to 1610. The house's own cigar brand, Meerapfel Cigars, was launched in 2021–2022)",
      founder: "Meir (Maier) Meerapfel (said to have lived 1847–1927), who founded the factory in 1876. The present heads of the house are the eleventh-generation brothers Joshua and Jeremiah Meerapfel, sons of Richard \"Rick\" Meerapfel, the man who established Cameroon wrapper leaf",
      meaning: "The family surname itself. A German-Jewish family name which reads literally as \"Meer (sea) + Apfel (apple)\", though no source clearly documenting the surname's origin has been found. In the cigar world the name has been made a brand as the very byword for Cameroon leaf",
      status: "Active, with two faces: (1) one of the world's largest growers and merchants of Cameroon leaf (said to contract with more than a thousand Cameroonian farmers in a given year), and (2) an ultra-premium house brand made in the Dominican Republic. In August 2025 the decades-old international distribution agreement with Arturo Fuente came to an end, and Fuente was reported to have switched its source of Cameroon leaf to Universal Leaf",
      history: "【The Cigar Industry's Unseen Protagonist — Leaf Merchant, Not Brand】\nFor a very long time the name Meerapfel was never printed on a cigar box or band. Yet within the industry the family has been known as one of the most important names of all. The reason is simple: they have in effect controlled the cultivation and distribution of the world's rarest wrapper leaf — African Cameroon. When countless devotees, never having heard the name, lit an Arturo Fuente Don Carlos or a Hemingway, the amber wrapper on those cigars was leaf that had passed through Meerapfel hands. In the cigar world there are families who leave their names as estate owners and factory masters, and families who reign as \"leaf merchants\", producing and procuring the leaf itself for supply to others. The Meerapfels are the exemplars of the latter — and because their chosen commodity was the rarest and most difficult leaf of all, Cameroon, they built a singular position that admits no rival. Not on the brand's front stage, but as suppliers of the leaf itself, propping up the industry's backbone — such has been the family's standing for generations.\n【Origins in the 1600s and the Untergrombach Factory (1876)】\nThe family's involvement with tobacco is very old indeed. Most overseas sources record that the Meerapfels were already growing and trading tobacco leaf in the 1600s (some accounts say 1610). The point of departure most widely cited for the modern business is 1876, the year Meir (Maier) Meerapfel (said to have lived 1847–1927, and sometimes styled \"Meir II\") established a cigar factory in Untergrombach, a village near Karlsruhe in south-western Germany. The firm is said at first to have supplied chewing tobacco to German miners, but the business gradually shifted its weight to hand-rolled cigar manufacture and then to the importing and brokering of leaf. As a German-Jewish family, they passed down from generation to generation the connoisseur's art of judging leaf quality. The house's proud claims today — \"a cigar family of eleven generations\", \"an involvement with tobacco reaching back to the 1600s\" — rest upon this long continuity, making it a lineage of unmatched antiquity in the premium cigar world.\n【The Era of Java and Sumatra Leaf, and Two World Wars】\nWith the twentieth century the family expanded abroad. From around 1900 they extended their sales network beyond Germany, and by 1933 Maier Meerapfel & Söhne is said to have become Germany's largest importer of Java and Sumatra wrapper. Indonesian leaf was then the leading wrapper for premium cigars, and the family rose to prominence on the strength of its discernment and procurement network. The Second World War, however, destroyed the German factory. As a Jewish family they were forced into dispersal, and some sources record that Ernest Meerapfel emigrated to the United States. After the war, the family left Europe behind yet continued processing and trading leaf, and were remembered as the pioneers who introduced Sumatra and Java leaf to Europe. That a family driven from Germany survived on tobacco leaf alone, changing origins and changing commodities, foreshadowed their later devotion to Cameroon.\n【Richard (Rick) Meerapfel and Cameroon】\nThe man who raised the family name to become the byword for Cameroon leaf was Richard \"Rick\" Meerapfel (1951–2003). The industry called him the \"miracle maker\" and regarded him as the world's foremost authority on, and largest supplier of, Cameroon wrapper. Cameroon leaf is thin and delicate, difficult both to grow and to ferment, and because it is produced in politically unstable Central Africa it has always lived next door to extinction. Rick staked his life on this fragile leaf. It is credited to him that its quality was raised a further degree and that this awkward wrapper was polished into one of the world's great leaves. In the early 1990s he accomplished what has been described as \"single-handedly saving Cameroon wrapper from the brink of extinction\", and his name was inscribed in cigar history. His untimely death in 2003 was mourned throughout the industry, and is the reason the family would later crown its finest line with the name \"Richard\".\n【SEITA's Withdrawal (1993) and CETAC】\nThe turning point came in 1993. SEITA, the French state tobacco monopoly which had long single-handedly administered the tobacco of Cameroon and the Central African Republic, withdrew from Central Africa on the grounds that the business no longer suited its objectives. The growing region lost its buyer and stood on the verge of collapse. It was here that Rick Meerapfel acted. In a joint venture with local Africans he founded CETAC (Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains, rendered as the Central African Tobacco Development Company), building an integrated structure for cultivation, contracting, processing and export. Though many around him tried to stop him, calling the venture sheer madness, he is said to have joined hands with the local people and rebuilt the collapsing industry itself. Thereafter the Meerapfels became the region's overwhelming supplier, said in some years to contract with more than a thousand Cameroonian farmers. To speak of genuine African-grown Cameroon leaf became, in effect, to speak of this family.\n【The Bond with the Fuentes — Don Carlos, Hemingway, and International Distribution】\nThe ties between Meerapfel and Arturo Fuente run deep. Rick supplied the Fuente family with Cameroon leaf for many years and is regarded as the moving spirit behind the creation of the first Don Carlos blend. Fuente's flagships — Don Carlos, Hemingway, Gran Reserva — all wear Cameroon wrapper, and the collaboration between the two houses is credited with influencing the entire cigar world. In addition, through a bonded warehouse in Belgium and a commercial office in Basel, Switzerland (Maier Meerapfel Söhne Belgium, MMS), the family handled the international distribution of Fuente products across Europe, the Middle East, Africa and Asia — everywhere except the United States and the Dominican Republic. They are also said to have handled European distribution for other marques such as Padrón. It should be noted that Fuente's Fuente Fuente OpusX \"Angel's Share\" is itself a cigar using Dominican rosado wrapper, its name said to derive from aged leaf lost in the 2012 warehouse fire, and is not directly connected with Cameroon leaf or with Meerapfel — but there is no doubt that the two houses' long association quietly sustained many of Fuente's masterpieces.\n【2025: The Parting with Fuente】\nIn August 2025, however, this relationship of many decades came to an end. Arturo Fuente announced that it was bringing international distribution in-house (through Fuente Marketing, with José Blanco as its representative) and dissolving its distribution agreement with Meerapfel. The focus fell on the fate of the Cameroon leaf: Meerapfel was its dominant supplier, and Fuente its greatest user. At first the heads of both houses declined to say whether supply would continue, but the statement issued by the Meerapfel side implied that the two were \"no longer in a business relationship\". Indeed, in November of that year Fuente announced Universal Leaf Tobacco Co. as its new source of Cameroon leaf, and leaf from that company was reported to have arrived at the Dominican factory. A partnership said to have lasted nearly half a century thus closed, in name and in fact. As an event touching the very structure of Cameroon leaf supply, the parting sent ripples through the industry.\n【The Birth of the House Brand, Meerapfel Cigars (2021–2022)】\nIt is only recently that the family of leaf merchants offered the world a cigar bearing its own name. In 2021 the eleventh-generation brothers Joshua and Jeremiah announced the brand's launch, and in October 2022 the first line, \"Richard\", was shipped. The cigars are made in the Dominican Republic, though the family steadfastly refuses to name the manufacturer. The wrapper is the family's prized Meerapfel Cameroon; the filler and binder are matched with extremely old, well-aged Dominican leaf. What caused particular excitement was the use of old aged Cameroon leaf carefully preserved since Rick's death. The supplier that had for so long quietly sustained the masterpieces of other houses had at last stepped onto the front stage — the raising of the house flag was its symbol.\n【The Master Blends — Meir, Ernest, Heller, Richard】\nThe house cigars comprise four pillars known as the \"Master Blends\", each dedicated to one generation of the family: the founder Meir; Ernest, who left Europe to search the world for leaf; Heller; and Richard. The Meir line offers a Double Robusto (52 x 146 mm), a Pirámide (52 x 156 mm), a Robusto (50 x 124 mm) and a Churchill (47 x 178 mm) among others, with slimmer sizes such as a Corona Gorda and a Lonsdale added later. The Ernest was unveiled at InterTabac in Germany in 2023, in Robusto, Corona Gorda, Double Robusto and Lonsdale sizes, with a Lancero added in 2025. Its flavour is described in terms of cedar, leather, espresso, dark chocolate and a faint spice. The Richard Double Robusto was crowned halfwheel's Cigar of the Year for 2023, and the Meir Robusto placed eighth on the same list. That a newly launched brand so soon earned the highest honours speaks to the quality of the leaf behind it.\n【The Ultra-Premium Course, and the Present】\nMeerapfel Cigars is known for some of the industry's most elaborate boxes and packaging, and for its high prices. The limited \"Création Liberté\" is said to cost roughly 240 US dollars for a set of two, positioning it less as a mere cigar than as an objet d'art and a luxury good. This ultra-premium course may be read as pricing in both the value of the rare Cameroon leaf and the family's long story. As leaf merchants they remain the linchpin of Cameroon supply, and instances have arisen of Meerapfel Cameroon leaf being supplied for other houses' new releases, such as E.P. Carrillo's \"Endure\" (2024). Cultivation and brokerage on the \"leaf side\", and an ultra-premium brand on the \"product side\" — the Meerapfel of today is that rare being which turns both wheels at once. Even after the parting with Fuente, the fate of the Cameroon leaf this family holds will continue to command the cigar world's attention.",
      vitolas: [
        "Meir — one of the Master Blends. Double Robusto (52 x 146 mm), Pirámide, Robusto, Churchill and others; Corona Gorda and Lonsdale added later",
        "Ernest — unveiled in 2023. Robusto, Corona Gorda, Double Robusto and Lonsdale, with a Lancero added in 2025",
        "Richard — shipped from October 2022. Double Robusto among others; halfwheel's 2023 Cigar of the Year",
        "Heller — a Master Blend dedicated to one generation of the family",
        "Création Liberté — a limited set of two cigars (said to cost about US$240)",
        "Meerapfel Cameroon wrapper (the house's principal commodity as leaf merchant, long supplied for Fuente's Don Carlos, Hemingway, Gran Reserva and others)",
        "E.P. Carrillo Endure (2024; an example of another house's cigar using Meerapfel Cameroon leaf)"
      ],
      trivia: "Richard Meerapfel is often described as \"the man who single-handedly saved Cameroon wrapper from extinction\". In the 1990s, when the French monopoly withdrew from Central Africa and the growing region teetered on the edge of collapse, he brushed aside those who called the venture reckless, founded a company with the local people, and protected this fragile, unforgiving leaf. After his death in 2003 the family continued to treasure and use the aged leaf he left behind, and crowned its finest line with his name, \"Richard\". Fittingly, the Double Robusto bearing that name was chosen as halfwheel's Cigar of the Year in 2023, the year after its 2022 debut."
    },
    "Cameroon Wrapper": {
      founded: "Commercial cultivation in the region dates from the early twentieth century (the transplanting of Sumatra seed). A full-scale production and export system under the French monopoly SEITA is held to have been established from the late 1950s onwards",
      founder: "No single founder. The foundations are credited to SEITA, the French state tobacco monopoly, and to the agronomist Jean Masseron, who established cultivation and fermentation techniques near Yaoundé at the end of the 1950s. The moving spirit behind the leaf's survival from the 1990s onwards was the leaf merchant Richard (Rick) Meerapfel",
      meaning: "\"Cameroon\" is the name denoting the wrapper leaf's origin and type. In practice it is the collective term for Central African wrapper grown in the Republic of Cameroon and the neighbouring Central African Republic; the parent seed variety is Sumatra",
      status: "Current but scarce. Genuine African-grown Cameroon ranks among the industry's most expensive and rarest wrappers, with the bulk of supply held by the Meerapfel family. Substitutes grown from Cameroon seed in Ecuador and elsewhere exist, but African-grown leaf is regarded as the true article",
      history: "【What Is \"Cameroon\"? — A Definition as a Place of Origin】\nIn the cigar world, \"Cameroon\" denotes not a particular brand but a wrapper leaf's origin and type. The leaf is actually grown in two Central African nations — the Republic of Cameroon and the neighbouring Central African Republic — and the produce of both is collectively called \"Cameroon wrapper\". The parent variety is Indonesia's Sumatra seed, to which the equatorial African soil and climate have given a character all its own. Thin, flecked with \"tooth\", sweet and spicy — it is a rare wrapper possessed of an individuality in a class apart among non-Cuban leaf. Production is limited, the genuine article is expensive, and it is difficult to handle. A cigar clothed in Cameroon therefore carries a certain distinction among the many wrappers of the world. Fine leaf is graded from \"grade 1\" downwards, and the higher the grade, the rarer and dearer the leaf is said to be.\n【The Transplanting of Sumatra Seed — Colonial Origins】\nThe story of Cameroon leaf begins in the colonial era. Overseas sources record that the Dutch brought Sumatra seed to Central Africa in the early twentieth century. Cameroon was originally a German possession (Kamerun), divided after the First World War into French and British mandate territories. Under French administration tobacco production developed in earnest, and the region's leaf began to supply the world's cigar industry. A Sumatra strain of Indonesian descent crossed to the distant equatorial belt of Central Africa and, bonding with that land's climate, transformed itself into a wrapper unlike any other — Cameroon leaf was, in part, an accidental product of colonial commerce. The territory's tangled history under Cameroon and the British mandate cast its shadow over the leaf's trade routes too, and is one reason why \"Cameroon\" as a place-name carries the ambiguity of straddling two nations.\n【SEITA and Jean Masseron — A Sunshade Made of Cloud】\nProduction was systematised by SEITA (Société d'exploitation industrielle des tabacs et des allumettes), the French state tobacco monopoly, which administered everything from cultivation to export in the region for roughly a century. At the end of the 1950s the monopoly dispatched the agronomist Jean Masseron, who is credited with opening trial fields near Yaoundé and establishing cultivation and fermentation techniques. What caught Masseron's attention was the region's characteristic layer of thin cloud. Because the cloud naturally softens the fierce equatorial sun, there is no need to stretch shade cloth (tents) as in other growing regions. This \"natural shade\" was the key that produced the thin, delicate, toothy Cameroon leaf. In offering the character of shade-grown leaf without the labour of erecting artificial shading, Cameroon was a rare and fortunate site bestowed by nature itself.\n【Post-Independence Disorder and Price Control】\nCameroon gained independence in 1960, but French influence over tobacco production long persisted. SEITA's fixed pricing and cumbersome bidding system became a factor that drove many cigar makers away. Because of this arrangement, criticised as \"unjust price inflation\", not a few makers in Central America — the Newman family among them — are said to have turned away from Cameroon leaf. The region lost its buyers' interest and slid into a vicious circle of shrinking plantings and declining quality. Excellent leaf though it was, rigid commerce and political circumstance obstructed its spread — the history of Cameroon is also a case study showing that leaf quality alone does not move a market.\n【The Leaf's Character — Thinness, Tooth, and Fragility】\nThe most striking visual feature of Cameroon leaf is its \"tooth\": a fine, granular, gooseflesh-like texture on the surface of the leaf, understood to be tiny pockets of concentrated tobacco oil just beneath the epidermis. Cameroon is rich in these oils and has a distinctive roughened feel. Thanks to the natural cloud-shade under which it grows, the colour finishes at a lighter to medium brown (colorado). The leaf, however, is exceedingly thin and brittle, and difficult to roll; it is said that only skilled torcedores can handle it, and this awkwardness is itself one cause of its scarcity. For the maker it is an expensive material with a poor yield, but that toothy appearance and rich oil are precisely what promise a gentle burn and depth of flavour. To choose Cameroon is to accept the cost and the labour for the sake of its character.\n【Flavour — A Lineage of Sweet Spice】\nOn the palate, the classic profile weds baking spices such as cinnamon and nutmeg, cedar, cocoa and nutty notes to a harmony of sweetness and a peppery tingle. The whole sits at medium body, distinguished by a toasty aroma and a lingering sweetness — a leaf that seduces not by strength but by the interplay of complexity and sweetness. Prized as an outstanding substitute for Cuban Corojo wrapper, it became from the 1970s onwards one of the standard wrappers for mild yet complex cigars. This lineage of sweet spice, so pronounced in Fuente's Hemingway and Don Carlos, is the very outline of the flavour most devotees picture when they hear the word Cameroon. The sweetness owes nothing to added sugar: it is the natural sweetness inherent in the leaf itself, and therein lies Cameroon's depth.\n【The Crisis of 1993 and the Brink of Extinction】\nIn the early 1990s Cameroon leaf faced annihilation. Waning interest brought reduced plantings and falling quality, until in 1993 SEITA halted the export of Cameroonian tobacco. This was the very moment the worldwide cigar boom was beginning, and by cruel irony the region came close to missing its greatest opportunity. Political instability and economic hardship compounded matters, and high-quality Cameroon wrapper was on the way to becoming \"a leaf soon to be unobtainable\". When the rigid commerce that lingered from long colonial rule collapsed, the region had neither the preparation nor the backing to stand alone — and that is what drove this leaf to the brink of extinction. It is said that the people of the region shrug off everything from foul weather to coups d'état with the single phrase \"C'est l'Afrique\" (\"That's Africa\") — such was the uncertainty with which cultivation lived side by side.\n【Revival under Meerapfel, and CETAC】\nThe man who rescued the leaf from this crisis was the leaf merchant Richard (Rick) Meerapfel. He stood his ground in the region after the French withdrawal, founding CETAC (rendered as the Central African Tobacco Development Company) in a joint venture with local Africans and rebuilding cultivation, contracting, processing and export as an integrated whole. While many around him warned that the venture was reckless, he is said to have joined hands with the local people and reconstructed the collapsing industry. Thereafter the supply of Cameroon leaf lay effectively in the hands of the Meerapfel family, who became an overwhelming presence contracting in some years with more than a thousand farmers. Most of the Cameroon wrapper we can obtain today stands in the lineage of that rescue. The history of Cameroon leaf is inseparable from the history of this family.\n【The Marques That Wear Cameroon — Fuente, Partagas and Others】\nThe most celebrated user of Cameroon leaf is Arturo Fuente. Hemingway, Don Carlos and Gran Reserva all wear this wrapper, and the rare Don Carlos edition \"Eye of the Shark\" was crowned Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year for 2017. The non-Cuban Partagas (General Cigar) is another standard-bearer: the story of Ramón Cifuentes choosing Cameroon for the wrapper of the first non-Cuban Partagas is famous, and the choice is carried on in Partagas 1845 and others. From the 1960s the leaf also served as the wrapper for comparatively large-production marques. More recently, E.P. Carrillo's \"Endure\" (2024, the house's first Cameroon since the 1970s) uses Meerapfel Cameroon leaf, and new adopters such as Aladino Cameroon (the Fernández family's JRE) have appeared — the return to this scarce leaf continues.\n【Cultivation and Harvest — Fields beneath the Clouds】\nThe cultivation of Cameroon leaf is sustained by the singular blessings of the equatorial belt. The soil is fertile and needs little fertiliser, temperature and humidity are favourable, and above all the thin cloud pours down diffused light. Under this diffused sunshine the leaf grows thin, supple and delicate. Harvesting is by priming — picking a few leaves at a time from the bottom of the plant upwards — and finishing a single plant is said to take about six weeks. The picked leaves are cured and matured over time from a paler shade into the rich brown called colorado. Each step of this painstaking process shapes the tooth and aroma peculiar to Cameroon. But Central Africa's unstable conditions have constantly menaced this patient fieldwork. That the road from field to cigar remains arduous and expensive is the fate of a land where nature's bounty and political hardship dwell together.\n【Scarcity, High Prices, and the Present】\nGenuine African-grown Cameroon is held to be one of the most expensive and rarest wrappers in the industry; grade 1 Cameroon is sometimes said to rank among the costliest wrapper leaf of all. Leaf raised from Cameroon seed in Ecuador and elsewhere exists, but the makers' common understanding is that the finer leaf is the \"genuine\" Cameroon grown in Africa. Owing to Central African instability, the road from seed to cigar remains difficult and dear. Even so, this sweet, toothy leaf raised beneath the clouds — with a singular character quite distinct from Cuban leaf — remains today a luxurious wrapper permitted only to a handful of great cigars. And by virtue of that very scarcity, Cameroon remains an object of longing for the connoisseur. The structure whereby one family holds most of the supply will surely continue to heighten both the leaf's rarity and its mystique. Not to confuse genuine African-grown leaf with \"Cameroon-seed\" leaf transplanted elsewhere is the first step towards understanding it.",
      vitolas: [
        "Arturo Fuente Hemingway (a representative masterpiece in Cameroon wrapper)",
        "Arturo Fuente Don Carlos (its \"Eye of the Shark\" was Cigar Aficionado's 2017 Cigar of the Year)",
        "Arturo Fuente Gran Reserva",
        "Partagas (non-Cuban, General Cigar; the leaf Ramón Cifuentes chose for the first non-Cuban Partagas)",
        "Partagas 1845",
        "E.P. Carrillo Endure (2024; uses Meerapfel Cameroon leaf)",
        "Aladino Cameroon (JRE, the Fernández family)",
        "The Meerapfel Cigars lines (wearing the house's own Meerapfel Cameroon wrapper)"
      ],
      trivia: "Cameroon leaf grows thin and delicate thanks, it is said, to the \"layer of thin cloud\" that covers this equatorial land. Where other regions must stretch shade cloth to block the direct sun, in Cameroon the natural cloud performs that office. Hence the character of shade-grown leaf is obtained without erecting any cloth, giving rise to the leaf's distinctive tooth and sweet-spiced aroma. That same thinness, however, is the roller's despair: it is said only veteran torcedores can handle it. When France withdrew in 1993 the leaf was driven to the very edge of extinction, but the tenacity of a single leaf merchant has kept this \"leaf raised by the clouds\" alive to the present day."
    },
    "CETAC (Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains)": {
      founded: "The early 1990s (the exact year of establishment is unknown). Founded by Richard \"Rick\" Meerapfel to prevent the supply of Cameroon leaf from being severed after SEITA, the French tobacco monopoly, withdrew from Cameroon. Some accounts date SEITA's withdrawal to 1993, others to \"the early 1990s\". By way of prehistory, Sumatra seed was brought to Cameroon by Dutch settlers in the early twentieth century, and industrialisation began when SEITA dispatched the agronomist Jean Masseron in the late 1950s to establish plantations",
      founder: "Richard \"Rick\" Meerapfel (1951–2003), head of the Meerapfel leaf-tobacco house founded in 1876 near Karlsruhe, Germany, by Maier Meerapfel, who for decades had purchased Cameroon leaf from SEITA through the Brussels firm M. Meerapfel & Söhne. CETAC was established as a private company jointly owned by the Meerapfel family and local African partners. After Rick's death in 2003 his sons Jeremiah and Joshua took over, and lead it to this day",
      meaning: "\"Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains\" is French for \"Central African Tobacco Development Company\". That the name invokes not \"Cameroon\" but \"Central Africa\" (Centrafricains) tells the true story: the leaf traded as \"Cameroon wrapper\" is in fact the produce of a belt stretching from eastern Cameroon into the western Central African Republic, and in the industry it is said the leaf \"should more accurately be called Central African\". The processing plants, too, lie mainly on the Central African Republic side (Gamboula, Berberati, Abba)",
      status: "Current. Two CETACs are said to exist, one each in Cameroon and the Central African Republic. Leaf is gathered from some 3,000 contract farmers (most of them smallholders with one to two acres), passes through central weighing stations, and is processed and fermented for export at the plants in Gamboula, Berberati and Abba in the Central African Republic. At the time of Rick Meerapfel's death the operation employed about 5,000 people. Supply remains tight — Carlos Fuente Jr has said that \"supply is still not sufficient\" — and in August 2020 a \"Seal of Authenticity\" for Meerapfel Cameroon leaf was introduced as a measure against counterfeiting",
      history: "【First, a Premise — Cameroon Is a Country of Leaf, Not of Brands】\nCameroon possesses, to all practical purposes, no internationally known premium cigar brand. And yet the country's name is printed on cigar boxes the world over. \"Cameroon wrapper\" — the distinctive, finely toothed wrapper leaf worn by Arturo Fuente's Don Carlos and Hemingway and by General Cigar's non-Cuban Partagas. The entity that actually grows that leaf in the fields, gathers it, ferments it and puts it on ships is CETAC, formally the Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains — the Central African Tobacco Development Company. It is telling that the name invokes not \"Cameroon\" but \"Central Africa\" (Centrafricains): though called \"Cameroon\" by trade custom, the leaf is in reality the produce of a belt stretching from eastern Cameroon into the western Central African Republic, and within the industry it is said that the leaf \"should more accurately be called Central African\". CETAC is a private company, jointly owned by the Meerapfel family and local African partners.\n\n【The French Colonial System and the SEITA Era】\nThe arrival of tobacco in Cameroon is an old story. The starting point is held to be the early twentieth century, when Dutch settlers brought Sumatra seed to Central Africa. It took industrial shape under the French colonial system: in the late 1950s SEITA, the French government's tobacco monopoly, established plantations in Cameroon. The man dispatched was the agronomist Jean Masseron, who perceived the region's decisive advantage: though it lies on the equator, a constant veil of thin cloud softens the sunlight, so that fine wrapper-grade leaf will grow without shade cloth — that is, sun-grown. Whereas Cuban and Connecticut wrappers are raised under tapado (shade cloth), Cameroon leaf became one of the few premium wrappers grown under the open sun. Cameroon gained independence in 1960, but real control of tobacco production long remained in French hands, under a system whereby SEITA bought up leaf from a multitude of local smallholders and exported it.\n\n【The Crisis of the 1990s — The Day France Left】\nThat system collapsed in the early 1990s, when SEITA withdrew from Cameroon (some accounts date the withdrawal to 1993). With the disappearance of the entity that had been at once buyer, technical adviser and logistics network, local tobacco production — no more than an aggregate of smallholders — should by rights have disintegrated. Indeed, by the mid-1990s Cameroon leaf was being described as \"endangered\".\nHere the Meerapfel family enters the story. The house began in 1876, when Maier Meerapfel supplied chewing tobacco to miners near Karlsruhe, Germany, and had been involved in growing, brokering and manufacture for generations since. Through the Brussels-based M. Meerapfel & Söhne, the family had for decades been the largest customer buying Cameroon leaf in quantity from SEITA. SEITA's withdrawal therefore meant, quite simply, the extinction of the family's trade.\nRichard \"Rick\" Meerapfel (1951–2003) would not sit and watch the supply die. He went in person into a region beset by civil war and unstable governance and established CETAC as a private company jointly owned by the Meerapfel family and local Africans. Contracting directly with the smallholders whose buyer had vanished, and assembling with his own hands everything from cultivation guidance to collection, fermentation and export — that is the making of the organisation called CETAC.\n\n【On the Ground — 3,000 Smallholders, Women's Hands, and Red-Earth Roads】\nCETAC's operating reality is far removed from the image of modern agribusiness. It contracts with some 3,000 farming households, most of whom can spare only one to two acres (0.4–0.8 hectares) for tobacco. Cultivation and harvesting are entirely by hand, and are carried out almost wholly by women. Curing, too, is done by each farmer at home. The dried leaf is brought to central weighing stations placed around the region, from which large four-wheel-drive lorries collect it and carry it to the processing plants at Gamboula, Berberati and Abba in the Central African Republic. A Cigar Aficionado field report records that most of the 1997 harvest had entered the fermentation bulks (stacked fermenting piles) at Gamboula and Berberati by September of that year, and that the half-fermented leaf was \"beautiful\". On the Cameroonian side, the establishment of a processing plant near the eastern town of Batouri (the East Region's second city after Bertoua, lying on the trunk road towards the Central African Republic) has been under discussion with the authorities. At the time of Rick Meerapfel's death, CETAC employed some 5,000 people. Today two CETACs exist, one each in Cameroon and the Central African Republic.\nThe difficulties of transport are likewise extraordinary. The field report tells of driving hundreds of kilometres over rutted red-earth roads, where demands for bribes at checkpoints manned by police carrying automatic rifles were an everyday matter. The words \"C'est l'Afrique\" (\"That's Africa\"), uttered by the guide Etienne Yandji in the village of Tapoulou, some twelve miles south of Berberati in the Central African Republic, became the title of that article — the local catch-phrase that explains everything from foul weather to coups d'état.\n\n【The Death of Rick Meerapfel, and Two Sons】\nRick Meerapfel died in 2003, aged 52. Carlos Fuente Jr has said: \"Without Rick there would be no more Cameroon wrapper. He saved this leaf from extinction. He went to Africa and saved the wrapper.\" Cigar Aficionado called him \"A Quiet Champion\", crediting him with building a worldwide reputation on superb tobacco while enriching the livelihoods of more than 100,000 people.\nImmediately after his death his two sons, Jeremiah and Joshua, took over the business, and they run CETAC to this day. Jeremiah later, as chairman of Meerapfel Cigars, also launched an ultra-premium cigar brand bearing the family name. Made in the Dominican Republic, it clothes aged fillers and binders in the family's own African Cameroon leaf; production is limited to 613 boxes per size per year, in what is called the industry's rarest and most expensive wrapper. Some twenty years after the father's death, the family that had supplied the leaf came to possess cigars of its own.\n\n【A Seal to Guarantee \"Genuine Cameroon\"】\nThe supply of Cameroon leaf remains tight today. Carlos Fuente Jr has said that \"supply is still not sufficient\", and the industry recognises that the leaf's journey from seed to cigar remains difficult and expensive owing to political instability in Cameroon and the Central African Republic. This has bred a further problem: products styling themselves \"Cameroon\" while actually using leaf of other origins.\nThe answer was the \"Seal of Authenticity\", announced in August 2020. A dedicated seal is affixed to boxes of Arturo Fuente products using the Meerapfel family's Cameroon leaf — the 8-5-8, Don Carlos and Hemingway lines among them — guaranteeing to the devotee that the wrapper is genuine; both Cigar Aficionado and Cigar Journal reported the scheme. Tying cultivation in the field to the seal on the box in a single unbroken line, the initiative shows that CETAC is not merely a producer of leaf but the custodian of the very concept of \"Cameroon wrapper\".\n\n【The Marques That Use Cameroon Leaf, and the Leaf's Character】\nThe leaf CETAC sends out is known for bearing, more richly than any other wrapper, the fine granular \"tooth\" — minute swellings formed where oils concentrate just beneath the leaf's surface. This tooth is held to be a source of flavour, yielding the character peculiar to Cameroon leaf: a toasty, nut-like sweetness with restrained spice. Products built upon this leaf include Arturo Fuente's 8-5-8, Don Carlos, Hemingway and Casa Fuente lines, General Cigar's non-Cuban Partagas (Ramón Cifuentes chose Cameroon for the original's wrapper), and Drew Estate's ACID. That this leaf — once ranked second in richness only to Cuba's Vuelta Abajo — still clothes the world's great cigars is owed to one man who, in the year of SEITA's withdrawal, went into the region and re-contracted with 3,000 smallholders. Though Cameroon has no cigar brand, this is why the country holds an assured seat in cigar history.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\n- The definition given in the commissioning note — \"an entity growing, processing and exporting Cameroon wrapper leaf in Cameroon and the Central African Republic\" — agrees with accounts in several specialist journals and has been corroborated. The formal name Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains is likewise confirmed.\n- CETAC's year of establishment could not be pinned down. SEITA's withdrawal is variously given as \"1993\" and \"the early 1990s\", and CETAC's founding is thought to have followed immediately, but no source giving a definitive year has been found.\n- Figures such as \"about 3,000 contract farmers\" and \"about 5,000 employees\" derive from Cigar Aficionado articles of the late 1990s to early 2000s and do not indicate present scale. No statistics on recent acreage, harvest or export volumes could be confirmed.\n- The processing plant near Batouri on the Cameroonian side was reported only as \"under discussion with the authorities\"; whether it came into operation is unconfirmed, as is the extent to which processing now takes place within Cameroon.\n- The statement that \"two CETACs exist, one each in Cameroon and the Central African Republic\" rests on Cigar Aficionado; details of the two companies' legal form and shareholdings could not be confirmed.\n- The present division of duties between the brothers Jeremiah and Joshua Meerapfel (which of them directs CETAC's day-to-day affairs) could not be determined.\n- The origin story that \"Dutch settlers brought Sumatra seed to Central Africa in the early twentieth century\" is widespread on industry reference sites, but has not been corroborated in primary sources.\n- No specific reporting could be found on production trends for Cameroon leaf in the 2020s (poor harvests, effects of civil conflict, rises or falls in supply). Statements about tight supply are quoted as general industry understanding.\n- Cameroon's domestic tobacco industry also includes cultivation and imports for cigarettes, but this entry is confined to premium cigar wrapper.",
      vitolas: [
        "[Leaf handled] Cameroon wrapper (the trade name; in reality Central African) — a sun-grown wrapper of Sumatra-seed descent, bearing the richest tooth (minute oil-formed swellings) of any wrapper",
        "[Leaf handled] Binder and filler leaf of the same origin — grown and first-processed in eastern Cameroon (around Batouri) and the western Central African Republic (around Berberati, Gamboula and Abba)",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Arturo Fuente 8-5-8 — a flagship line in Cameroon wrapper; bears the Seal of Authenticity since 2020",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Arturo Fuente Don Carlos — likewise in Cameroon wrapper; covered by the Seal of Authenticity",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Arturo Fuente Hemingway — the celebrated figurado-centred line; Cameroon wrapper, covered by the Seal of Authenticity",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Casa Fuente — the Las Vegas-exclusive Fuente line; uses Meerapfel Cameroon leaf",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Partagas (non-Cuban, General Cigar) — Ramón Cifuentes selected Cameroon for the wrapper of the original non-Cuban Partagas",
        "[Marques using the leaf] ACID (Drew Estate) — includes products in Cameroon wrapper",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Meerapfel Cigars — the ultra-premium family-name brand launched by Jeremiah Meerapfel; made in the Dominican Republic, wearing the family's exclusive African Cameroon leaf, with production limited to 613 boxes per size per year"
      ],
      trivia: "CETAC's fieldwork is carried out almost entirely by women. Cultivation and harvesting are wholly by hand, and each farmer cures the leaf at home. The dried leaf is gathered at central weighing stations around the region, whence large four-wheel-drive lorries drive hundreds of kilometres over rutted red-earth roads to the processing plants in the Central African Republic — and along the way, a Cigar Aficionado field report records, demands for bribes at checkpoints manned by police with automatic rifles were an everyday matter. The article's title, \"C'est l'Afrique\" (\"That's Africa\"), came from the words of the guide Etienne Yandji in the village of Tapoulou, south of Berberati in the Central African Republic — the local catch-phrase that accounts for every absurdity from foul weather to coups d'état. The same magazine called Rick Meerapfel \"A Quiet Champion\", crediting him with building a worldwide reputation on superb tobacco while enriching the lives of more than 100,000 people. Carlos Fuente Jr put it more bluntly: \"Without Rick there would be no more Cameroon wrapper. He went to Africa and saved the wrapper.\""
    },
    "SEITA (Société d'Exploitation Industrielle des Tabacs et des Allumettes)": {
      founded: "Established as the \"Service d'Exploitation Industrielle des Tabacs\" (SEIT) by decree of 13 August 1926. The match monopoly was added in 1935, making it SEITA, and in 1959 its legal form was changed from \"Service\" to \"Société\". The cultivation venture in Cameroon was launched in the 1950s",
      founder: "The French state. A state tobacco and match monopoly with no individual founder. Some sources name the agronomist Jean Masseron as the man who directed the trial fields in Cameroon, though corroboration is limited",
      meaning: "\"Industrial Operating Company for Tobaccos and Matches\". The words \"et des Allumettes\" were appended when the match (allumettes) manufacturing monopoly was added in 1935. In the French-speaking world the four-letter abbreviation itself came to stand for \"French tobacco\"",
      status: "Defunct. It ceased its Central African operations in 1993, was privatised in 1995, merged with Spain's Tabacalera in 1999 to form Altadis, and Altadis was acquired by Imperial Tobacco of Britain in 2008",
      history: "【The State in Cigar History】\nThe history of cigars is usually told as a story of families and factories: the Fuentes, the Padróns, the Meerapfels. But where Cameroon wrapper — one of the world's scarcest leaves — is concerned, the centre of the story is occupied not by a family but by a state. For roughly half of the twentieth century, the cultivation, purchase and export of this leaf were held in one hand by SEITA, the French government's tobacco monopoly. Neither in Cameroon nor in the Central African Republic did any company exist to sell the leaf to the world on its own account. In a warehouse in Paris, prices were settled by sealed bids, and the leaf passed out of the local farmers' hands. Behind the aroma of Cameroon leaf lies the institutional memory of this state monopoly.\n\n【From SEIT to SEITA — The Lineage of the French Tobacco Monopoly】\nThe French tobacco monopoly is ancient, reaching back to the royal monopolies of the seventeenth century. As a modern organisation its point of departure was the decree of 13 August 1926 creating the \"Service d'Exploitation Industrielle des Tabacs\" (SEIT). In 1935 the manufacture of matches (allumettes) was placed under its monopoly, adding \"et des Allumettes\" to the name and making it SEITA. In 1959 its legal form was changed from \"Service\" to \"Société\", and thereafter the four letters became synonymous with French tobacco itself. SEITA retained the monopoly of leaf-tobacco growing in France until 1970, and of tobacco and match manufacture and sale until 1976. At the end of the 1950s the monopoly system is said to have sustained some 100,000 leaf-tobacco farming households, 11,000 SEITA staff, technicians and workers, and more than 50,000 tobacconists. Gauloises and Gitanes — both born in 1910 — were the two emblematic marques on the company's signboard.\n\n【The 1950s: To the Equatorial Fields】\nSEITA reached out to Cameroon in the 1950s. After the First World War the former German colony of Cameroon had been divided between France and Britain as League of Nations mandates, with the eastern half under French administration. The leaf destined for cigars is held to have taken root in Cameroon and neighbouring Central Africa with Sumatra seed brought by the Dutch in the early twentieth century. The French monopoly saw the land's promise and launched a full cultivation venture in the 1950s. One source names the agronomist Jean Masseron as the director of trial fields near Yaoundé, and records that the region's singular climate — thin cloud constantly tempering the sunlight even on the equator — suited the production of delicate wrapper leaf. Cultivation of Cameroon leaf did indeed spread around Batouri in the east and, across the border, through the Gamboula, Berberati and Abba districts of the Central African Republic.\n\n【The Sealed Bids of Paris】\nThe trading mechanism SEITA established was peculiar — and would later provoke deep dissatisfaction. Leaf was sold by silent bidding with sealed bids, centred on SEITA's warehouse at Nanterre outside Paris. Buyers could participate only through brokers, with up to three chances to bid on each lot. All they were given was a catalogue of bound samples; they could not inspect the actual bales with their own eyes. Complaints from the buyers' side never ceased: \"the French keep the best leaf for themselves\"; \"the prices are too high\". For large cigar manufacturers needing hundreds of bales a year, a system that guaranteed neither quality nor price was intolerable, and by the early 1980s the big buyers were withdrawing one after another. What the state monopoly produced was not stability of supply but opacity of supply.\n\n【The Withdrawal of 1993】\nThen, in 1993, SEITA ceased operations in the Central African region. The stated reason was that \"demand no longer exists, and this business does not accord with SEITA's objectives\". France stopped exporting leaf tobacco from Africa. No timing could have been worse. The early 1990s came immediately before the \"cigar boom\", the explosion of demand for premium cigars centred on the United States. At the precise moment the world began to need fine wrapper leaf most, the supply chain for Cameroon leaf was severed by a single decision of state. Into this void leapt the Belgium-based leaf merchant Rick Meerapfel, and the company he founded with the local people was CETAC (Compagnie d'Exploitation des Tabacs Centrafricains). The entries in this encyclopaedia for \"CETAC\" and \"the Meerapfel family\" tell the story from 1993 onwards. The SEITA entry concerns the forty years before.\n\n【The Final Act at Home — Privatisation and Altadis】\nAround the time it withdrew from Africa, SEITA itself was slipping from the state's hands. In July 1984 the French government made SEITA an independent company, though the state retained all the shares. Privatisation was announced at the end of 1994, and between 7 and 14 February 1995 the shares were offered to the public — a great success, taken up by a million individual investors. In 1999 SEITA merged with its Spanish counterpart Tabacalera to create Altadis, and Altadis was acquired by Imperial Tobacco of Britain in 2008. That a company born of the union of two state monopolies, French and Spanish, should end under British ownership offers a panorama of how the institution of the state tobacco monopoly in twentieth-century Europe was dismantled. Altadis also came to hold 50% of the shares of Cuba's Habanos S.A., connecting it to yet another centre of the cigar world.\n\n【What Remains】\nSEITA left no name as a cigar brand. Cigars using Cameroonian leaf circulate the world under the marques of other nations: Arturo Fuente's Don Carlos and Hemingway, Partagas, Ashton, Macanudo, CAO, Hoyo de Monterrey. Yet without SEITA's forty years, the commercial category of \"Cameroon leaf\" would never have come into being. It was this monopoly that introduced the cultivation techniques, organised the farmers, built the processing and export system, and shaped the leaf into a form the cigar makers of Europe and America could buy. It is equally true that its monopolistic, opaque trading system drove buyers away, and that its abrupt withdrawal in 1993 pushed the industry to the brink of extinction. When Cameroon wrapper is called \"the leaf that nearly vanished\", the crisis was made neither by blight nor by climate, but by a single management decision taken in Paris. The archives of the French Ministry of the Economy and Finance preserve audit records of SEITA and its subsidiaries, and the SEITA photographic collection is open to the public. The record of the age when the state ran tobacco is preserved not as corporate history but as administrative history.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\n- The precise year SEITA entered Cameroon could not be determined; accounts say only \"the late 1950s\" or \"the 1950s\".\n- The statement that the agronomist Jean Masseron directed trial fields near Yaoundé rests on a single cigar-trade publication and has not been corroborated elsewhere.\n- The account that \"the Dutch brought Sumatra seed to Central Africa in the early twentieth century\" is widely shared in the cigar trade, but primary sources have not been verified.\n- Sources waver on the sequence of name changes. Explanations that \"it became SEIT in 1935 and then SEITA when the match monopoly was added\" coexist with those that \"in 1959 it was renamed from Service to Société\"; this entry records both.\n- The description of the Paris bidding venue as \"the warehouse at Nanterre\" rests on a Cigar Aficionado article; whether other venues existed is unknown.\n- The reason for the 1993 withdrawal rests on SEITA's own explanation of \"declining demand and mismatch with company objectives\"; the true substance of the decision cannot be verified.\n- Quantitative figures — direct employment, acreage, export volumes in Cameroon and Central Africa — could not be confirmed.\n- The precise legal form of SEITA's monopoly over the Cameroon leaf trade (state monopoly, exclusive purchase contract, colonial administrative designation or otherwise) has not been established.",
      vitolas: [
        "[Leaf handled] Cameroonian wrapper leaf (around Batouri in the east) and Central African Republic wrapper leaf (the Gamboula, Berberati and Abba districts) — SEITA monopolised the supply of the leaf known collectively in the trade as \"Cameroon wrapper\" from the 1950s until 1993",
        "[Leaf handled] Strains descending from the Sumatra seed said to have been brought to Central Africa by the Dutch in the early twentieth century",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Arturo Fuente — Don Carlos, Hemingway (the standard-bearers of Cameroon leaf)",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Partagas (General Cigar's non-Cuban version), Ashton, Macanudo, CAO, Hoyo de Monterrey",
        "[Own products] Gauloises and Gitanes — the emblematic French cigarette marques, both born in 1910",
        "[Own products] Matches (allumettes); the manufacturing monopoly added in 1935 became part of the company's name",
        "[Trading mechanism] Sealed (silent) bidding centred on the warehouse at Nanterre outside Paris; buyers saw only sample catalogues and could bid, through brokers, up to three times per lot"
      ],
      trivia: "SEITA's withdrawal in 1993 could not have been worse timed. The early 1990s came immediately before the \"cigar boom\", the American-led explosion in demand for premium cigars — and at the precise moment the world began to need fine wrapper leaf most, the supply chain for Cameroon leaf was cut by a single decision of state. The reason Cameroon wrapper came to be called \"the leaf that nearly vanished\" was neither blight nor climate change, but a management decision taken in Paris."
    },
    "Universal Leaf Tobacco Co. (Universal Corporation)": {
      founded: "25 January 1918, when the Virginia State Corporation Commission issued the charter of incorporation. Formed by the amalgamation of six leaf-tobacco dealers, including the J.P. Taylor Company, whose origins reach back to the late nineteenth century",
      founder: "No single founder. The six amalgamating firms were C.B. Cheatham & Co. (Farmville, North Carolina), the Gorman Tobacco Company (Hopkinsville, Kentucky), the J.P. Taylor Company and the Oscar C. Gregory Tobacco Company (both of Richmond, Virginia), W.A. Willingham & Co. (Danville, Virginia), and W.H. Winstead & Co. (Baltimore and elsewhere)",
      meaning: "\"Universal\" (all-embracing) + \"leaf tobacco\". The name proclaims the founding purpose of the six-firm amalgamation: to create a leaf-tobacco dealer of national scale. The holding company is styled Universal Corporation",
      status: "In operation. A listed company headquartered in Richmond, Virginia, with operations in more than thirty countries — the world's largest independent leaf-tobacco merchant. Revenue for the 2025 financial year was US$2.9 billion (up 7% year on year), with operating profit of US$232 million (up 5%)",
      history: "【The \"Invisible Company\" That Underpins the Cigar Industry】\nFrom November 2025, the Cameroon leaf wrapped around Arturo Fuente's Don Carlos began arriving by the hand of a different company — and for the cigar industry, the news meant far more than a change of supplier. For the first time, the position of the Meerapfel family, which for thirty years had held the worldwide distribution of Cameroon leaf almost entirely in its grasp, had been shaken. The company that appeared in its place was Universal Leaf Tobacco Co.: the world's largest independent leaf-tobacco merchant, headquartered in Richmond, Virginia. With operations in more than thirty countries across five continents, it is nonetheless almost unknown to the ordinary consumer. Its name is never printed on a cigar box. And yet there are parts of the cigar industry that simply do not turn without it.\n\n【1918: The Amalgamation of Six Firms】\nThe origins of Universal Corporation reach back to the J.P. Taylor Company of the late nineteenth century. On 25 January 1918 the Virginia State Corporation Commission issued a charter of incorporation to the Universal Leaf Tobacco Company. It was the product of an amalgamation of six leaf-tobacco dealers: C.B. Cheatham & Co. of Farmville, North Carolina; the Gorman Tobacco Company of Hopkinsville, Kentucky; the J.P. Taylor Company and the Oscar C. Gregory Tobacco Company, both of Richmond, Virginia; W.A. Willingham & Co. of Danville, Virginia; and W.H. Winstead & Co., with bases in Baltimore, Goldsboro and Owensboro. The leaf dealer (or leaf merchant) buys leaf from farmers, sorts it, cures and ferments it, packs it, stores it, and sells it to the makers of cigarettes, cigars and pipe tobacco. Neither growing nor selling to the public, it is a trade that stands between production and manufacture — and this unglamorous calling was the backbone of the twentieth-century tobacco industry.\n\n【A Procurement Network of Global Scale】\nToday's Universal Corporation handles every major type of leaf tobacco: flue-cured Virginia, Burley, dark air-cured, dark fire-cured and Oriental. In Africa it has operations in Malawi (through the subsidiary Limbe Leaf Tobacco, 58% Universal and 42% the local Press Corporation), Mozambique, South Africa and Zimbabwe; in Asia, with Singapore as regional headquarters, in the Philippines, Indonesia, China, India and Bangladesh; in Europe in Italy, Spain, Hungary, Poland and the Netherlands; and in North America in the United States, Canada, Guatemala and Mexico. In recent years it has added an \"Ingredients\" division dealing in plant-derived materials — fruit, vegetable and botanical extracts and flavourings — and in the 2025 financial year it recorded revenue of US$2.9 billion (up 7% year on year) and operating profit of US$232 million (up 5%). This is a scale of operations in an altogether different order from the premium cigar trade.\n\n【Dark Air-Cured — The Division That Touches Cigars】\nUniversal touches the cigar world chiefly through its \"dark tobacco\" operations. Dark air-cured and dark fire-cured leaf supplies the raw material for cigars, natural-wrapped cigars and cigarillos, smokeless tobacco and pipe tobacco. By the company's own account, dark tobacco is grown in the United States, Indonesia, the Dominican Republic, Brazil, Paraguay and Ecuador. In the Dominican Republic, INETAB-KAUBECK of Santiago handles procurement and sales; in Brazil, Universal Leaf Tabacos and Ermor Tabarama Tabacos do Brasil procure, process and sell in Rio Grande do Sul and Bahia. The presence in Bahia — that is, the Recôncavo Baiano — shows that this merchant house is woven into the raw-material supply network of the Brazilian cigar as well. Ecuador, too, is a region indispensable to the cigar industry for its Connecticut Shade and Habano wrappers.\n\n【2025: A Thirty-Year Monopoly Breaks】\nIn August 2025 it was announced that Arturo Fuente and M. Meerapfel Söhn were ending their long-standing distribution partnership, with Fuente's international distribution to be handled in-house thereafter. In September the two houses' formal severance of business relations was confirmed, and the industry's attention converged on a single question: where would Fuente obtain its Cameroon leaf? Don Carlos, Hemingway and the company's other flagship marques are built upon Cameroon wrapper, and the Meerapfel family held almost the entire world supply of the leaf. The answer came in November of that year. Fuente announced Universal Leaf Tobacco as its new source of Cameroon leaf, and Carlos Fuente Jr told Cigar Aficionado at the Big Smoke in Las Vegas that supply was well assured for years to come. A forty-foot container of African-grown Cameroon leaf was reported to have arrived at the company's Dominican factory, with another to follow in December.\n\n【What Has Changed】\nThe significance of the affair goes beyond one company's change of supplier. Ever since France's SEITA withdrew in 1993, Cameroon leaf had lain effectively under the exclusive management of the Meerapfel family, who had organised CETAC on the ground, contracted with some 3,000 smallholders, and handled everything from cultivation to processing and export as a single chain. Into that structure the world's largest independent leaf merchant has now entered by a separate route. How Universal secures its Cameroon leaf — whether it grows and buys on the ground itself, or merely purchases from existing producers and distributes — has not been disclosed, and Cameroon does not appear in the company's published list of country operations. Yet since the concrete tonnage of a \"forty-foot container\" has moved, there can be no doubt that a supply chain exists. The structure surrounding Cameroon leaf may be shifting from single-house monopoly to plural channels of distribution.\n\n【The Weight of the Leaf Merchant's Trade】\nIn the premium cigar world, brands and factories are talked about; leaf merchants are not. Yet in reality it is the leaf merchant who decides which field's leaf reaches which factory. Where the Meerapfels have held Cameroon leaf as a leaf-merchant dynasty of eleven generations, Universal — born of a six-firm amalgamation in 1918 — is a listed company that has moved the world's leaf on the scale of the cigarette industry. Both are covered by the term \"leaf merchant\", but their characters are utterly different: the one a family firm specialised in small-volume, high-value wrapper leaf; the other an agricultural commodities house handling crops by the hundreds of thousands of tonnes. That the latter has now laid its hand on premium cigars' most storied raw material may mean that this small industry is being drawn into the logic of the wider agricultural commodity trade. What that will mean for the 3,000 farming households tilling their one or two acres in Cameroon, no one yet knows.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\n- How Universal Leaf secures its Cameroon leaf (own cultivation and purchase, or resale from existing producers) has not been disclosed and could not be confirmed. Cameroon is absent from the company's published list of country operations.\n- Accordingly, the judgement that \"the Meerapfel family's thirty-year monopoly has broken\" is an interpretation of the picture presented by press reports, not a verified account of the supply chain; the possibility that Universal is supplied by Meerapfel and reselling cannot be excluded.\n- Neither Arturo Fuente nor the Meerapfel family has disclosed the reason for their break.\n- The statement that \"the Meerapfel family held almost the entire world supply of Cameroon leaf\" rests on industry media; the precise market share has not been confirmed.\n- The scale, duration and terms of the Universal–Fuente arrangement have not been made public; all that has been reported is an initial shipment of two forty-foot containers.\n- Some search results describe \"Limbe Leaf Tobacco\" as the company's Cameroonian subsidiary; this is an error. Limbe Leaf Tobacco is a Malawian subsidiary, Limbe being a Malawian city (the confusion apparently arising because Cameroon has a city of the same name).\n- The share of cigar-grade dark tobacco in the company's overall business could not be confirmed; the bulk of revenue appears to derive from flue-cured and Burley, that is, cigarette leaf.\n- The financial figures for the 2025 financial year are drawn from results announcements; whether the Cameroon business is reflected in them is unknown.",
      vitolas: [
        "[Leaf handled] Flue-cured Virginia / Burley / dark air-cured / dark fire-cured / Oriental — every major type of leaf tobacco",
        "[Cigar-related leaf] Dark air-cured and dark fire-cured, the raw material for cigars, natural-wrapped cigars and cigarillos, smokeless tobacco and pipe tobacco",
        "[Dark tobacco origins] The United States, Indonesia, the Dominican Republic, Brazil, Paraguay and Ecuador",
        "[Operating company] INETAB-KAUBECK, SRL (Santiago, Dominican Republic; procurement and sales)",
        "[Operating companies] Universal Leaf Tabacos Ltda. and Ermor Tabarama Tabacos do Brasil Ltda. (Brazil, Rio Grande do Sul and Bahia; procurement, processing and sales)",
        "[Operating company] Limbe Leaf Tobacco Company Limited (Malawi; 58% Universal, 42% the local Press Corporation — not a Cameroonian company)",
        "[Cameroon leaf from 2025] Supplier of Cameroon wrapper to Arturo Fuente; the first consignment (one forty-foot container) reported to have arrived at the company's Dominican Republic factory in November 2025, with a second due in December",
        "[Marques using the leaf] Arturo Fuente — Don Carlos, Hemingway (the marques that had hitherto used Cameroon leaf supplied via the Meerapfel family)",
        "[Non-tobacco business] The Ingredients division (plant-derived materials such as fruit, vegetable and botanical extracts and flavourings)"
      ],
      trivia: "This company's name is never printed on a cigar box. Yet the Cameroon leaf supply agreement of November 2025 meant that, for the first time since France's SEITA withdrew in 1993, a channel other than the Meerapfel family's near-monopoly had appeared in the distribution of Cameroon leaf. Incidentally, the Limbe Leaf Tobacco company that often surfaces in searches as \"Universal's Cameroonian subsidiary\" is a Malawian firm — a confusion arising from the fact that Cameroon also has a city called Limbe."
    }
  };
  BRANDS_DATA.cameroon.forEach(function (b) {
    var e = EN[b.en];
    if (!e) return;
    if (e.vitolas && b.vitolas && e.vitolas.length !== b.vitolas.length) delete e.vitolas;
    Object.assign(b, e);
  });
})();
